Blender-to-JSON Room Export Instructions



Purpose:
	
	Explain how to create environment/scene models in Blender.
	
	The OBJ export contains only visible room geometry.
	
	The JSON export contains gameplay/helper data such as:
		player spawn
		fixed cameras
		camera zones
		colliders
		door triggers
		interaction triggers
		point lights
		spot lights
		light flicker settings
		ambient room color



Coordinate system conversion:

	The engine converts Blender coordinates as:
		engine = glm::vec3(blender.x, blender.z, -blender.y);

	This means that:
		Blender X -> engine X
		Blender Y -> engine -Z
		Blender Z -> engine Y

	So:
		Blender floor plane is X/Y
		Blender height is Z
		Engine floor plane is X/Z
		Engine height is Y

	The RoomLoader converts the coordinates of the helper objects from Blender space to engine space:
		playerSpawn
		camera position
		camera target
		camera zones
		colliders
		doors
		door targetSpawn
		interactions
		light positions
		spotlight targets

	The loader does not do any conversion on other data such as:
		colors, ambientColor, texture paths, model paths, light intensity and etc...



Script configuration:

	You should check the initial config values at the top of the 
	script everytime before running it for a specific room.

	ROOM_ID

		Change this per room: ROOM_ID = [some name]
		Examples:
		    ROOM_ID = "testRoom"
		    ROOM_ID = "Room01"
			ROOM_ID = "room02"

		This variable is used to generate paths like:
			MODEL_PATH = f"src/models/{ROOM_ID}/{ROOM_ID}.obj" 
			JSON_PATH = f"src/models/{ROOM_ID}/{ROOM_ID}.json"

		Make sure the folder name, OBJ name, MTL name, and JSON path match exactly.
		Linux paths are case-sensitive.


	CAMERA_NAMES:

		Set the cameras used by the room:
			e.g CAMERA_NAMES = ["Cam01", "Cam02"]
		
		Make sure to follow the format of two digits and start from 01:
			Cam01, Cam02, Cam03, Cam04, Cam05, Cam06, Cam07, Cam08, Cam09, Cam10

		Each camera should have three helper objects in Blender (add them like regular objects with Ctrl + A):
			Cam01_Pos
			Cam01_Target
			Cam01_Zone


	ROOM_WALLS_OBJECT:
		
		Set ROOM_WALLS_OBJECT to match the mesh used to calculate outer wall bounds.
			e.g ROOM_WALLS_OBJECT = "room_walls"



Blender Naming Convention

	Required / Common helpers:

		PlayerSpawn

		Cameras:
			Cam[number e.g 0]_Pos
			Cam[number e.g 0]_Target
			Cam[number e.g 0]_Zone

		Colliders:
			Any mesh object whose name starts wih Col_:
				e.g Col_Tree, Col_Table, Col_Blocker_01

		Doors:
			Door_01
			Door_Hallway
			Door_01_Spawn and etc.

		Interactions:
			Interact_TestNote
			Interact_WallMessage
			Interact_* and etc.

		Lights:
			Point lights: Light_01, Light_02, Light_Hallway

			Spot lights:	Light_Storm, Light_Ceiling 
							Light_Storm_Target, Light_Ceiling_Target

			Important:	
				Spot lights must have a target object named with the suffix "_Target" in Blender 
						e.g Light_Storm_Target. 
				If _Target is misssing then the script will consider the target as a point light 
				and will not export the direction vector to the JSON.
		


Door setup:

	Create a cube where the player should press E. 
	Name it Door_[some name]
		e.g Door_01

	Add custom property that points to the next room:
			e.g targetRoom = src/models/room02/room02.json

	You can also create an empty or helper object named Door_[some name]_Spawn so that the player 
	appears in that specific location in the next room.

	Important:
		Blender Z will be converted to engine Y by the RoomLoader, 
		so the height of the trigger zone should be controlled with Blender Z.
		Door cubes are JSON helper only.
		Do not export door helper cubes in the OBJ. 
		Only the visible room geometry should be exported in the OBJ.



Camera setup:

	For each camera, create three helper objects in Blender:
		Cam[number e.g 01]_Pos
		Cam[number e.g 01]_Target
		Cam[number e.g 01]_Zone

	Cam[number]_Pos is the camera position in Blender space.
	Cam[number]_Target is the camera target in Blender space, where the camera is looking.
	Cam[number]_Zone is the trigger zone where this camera becomes active once the player enters it.

	A custom property for field of view (fov) can be added to the camera position or zone object:
		e.g fov = 90.0

	If no fov is specified, the script will use a default value of 95.0 degrees.



Interaction setup:
	
	Interaction helpers are invisible trigger zones.
	They are included in the JSON but not exported in the OBJ.

	Message Interaction:
		Create a cube and name it Interact_[some name]
			e.g Interact_WallMessage
		Add custom properties:
			id = wall_message
			type = message
			message = The wall is covered in strange marks.
			repeatable = true

	Note Image Interaction:
		Create a cube and name it Interact_[some name]
			e.g Interact_TestNote
		Add custom properties:
			id = test_note
			type = note
			asset = src/ui/notes/test_note.png
			repeatable = true
		The note appears as a UI overlay when the player presses E inside the interaction zone.



Point Light setup:

	Point lights are used for lamps, bulbs, candles, glowing notes, and general local light sources.

	1. Add point light: 
		Add -> Light -> Point

	2. Rename it
		e.g Light_01

	3. Position it 

	4. Add custom properties:
		type = point
		color = 1.0, 0.75, 0.45
		intensity = 4.0
		radius = 14.0
		flicker = false

	If you do not add custom properties, the script will refer to default values)



Spot Light setup:

	Spot lights are used for directed light:
		window lightning
		flashlights
		light beams
		targeted horror lightning
		and etc...

	1. Create two objects:
		Light_Storm
		Light_Storm_Target

		Light_Storm is the light source
		Light_Storm_Target is the target where the light aims.

		The target object must be named with the suffix "_Target" in Blender and not be exported as real light source. 
		The script itself skips objects with the suffix "_Target" initially 
		and later adds it to the output JSON as a property for the spot light.

	2. Add custom properties to the light source object:
		type = spot
		color = 1.0, 0.75, 0.45
		intensity = 10.0
		radius = 25.0
		innerCutoff = 12.0
		outerCutoff = 28.0
		flicker = true
		flickerType = lightning
		flickerFrequency = 0.25
		flickerStrength = 1.0
		flickerRandomness = 1.0
		flickerThreshold = 0.93

	Cutoff values:
		innerCutoff = angle in degrees where the light is at full intensity
		outerCutoff = angle in degrees where the light is at zero intensity
		The engine will interpolate the light intensity between inner and outer cutoff angles.

		For narrow light beams:
			innerCutoff = 8.0
			outerCutoff = 18.0

		For medium light cone:
			innerCutoff = 18.0
			outerCutoff = 45.0

		For wide light cone:
			innerCutoff = 45.0
			outerCutoff = 80.0

		For debugging purposes, you can use wider values and narrow them down later.


Light flicker setup:
	
	Lights can use different flicker modes.

	Flicker Properties:
		enabled = bool (whether the light flickers or not)
		type = "flicker type" (the nature of the flicker, e.g lightning, old halogen tube)
		frequency = .float value (how often the light flickers, in seconds)
		strength = .float value (amount of intensity variation)
		randomness = .float value (how random the flicker is)
		threshold = .float value (mainly used for lightning flicker)

	Flicker Types:

		Normal / Sine:
			Simple smooth flicker. Default type.

				flicker = true 
				flickerType = sine 
				flickerFrequency = 8.0 
				flickerStrength = 0.2

		Fluorescent:
			Fast, nervous office light. Can simulate old fluorescent tubes. 
				flicker = true 
				flickerType = fluorescent 
				flickerFrequency = 16.0 
				flickerStrength = 0.18 
				flickerRandomness = 0.05

		Tungsten:
			Low-voltage incandescent wobble. Can simulate old tungsten bulbs. 
				flicker = true 
				flickerType = tungsten 
				flickerFrequency = 4.0 
				flickerStrength = 0.35 
				flickerRandomness = 0.35

		Lightning:
			Mostly dark, with occasional bright flashes.
				flicker = true 
				flickerType = lightning 
				flickerFrequency = 0.25 
				flickerStrength = 1.0 
				flickerRandomness = 1.0 
				flickerThreshold = 0.93

			Higher threshold means rarer flashes.
				threshold = 0.90 -> more frequent 
				threshold = 0.96 -> rarer



Ambient color setup:
	
	Ambient color is set as a custom property on the Blender scene.

	To set the ambient color in a room:
		1. Go to Scene and add a custom property (can be a float array)

		2. Name it "ambientColor"

		3. Set the values
			(if you do not add custom ambient color the script will use default values)

	Normal dim room:
		ambientColor = 0.12, 0.12, 0.14

	Warm old room:
		ambientColor = 0.16, 0.12, 0.09

	Cold blue room:
		ambientColor = 0.08, 0.10, 0.15

	Very dark room at night:
		ambientColor = 0.04, 0.04, 0.05



Important helper object rules:
	
	For all 
		Cam*_Zone 
		Col_* 
		Door_* 
		Interact_* 
		Light_*_Target

	follow these rules:
		
		1. Keep them axis-aligned for now
		2. Apply transforms: Ctrl + A -> Rotation & Scale!!!!
		3. Make zones and doors tall in Blender Z, because Blender Z will be transformer to Y by the engine
		4. Use helper objects for JSON generation only. They should not be exported in the OBJ.
		5. Light target helpers should not have light custom properties.
		6. Light targets should not be exported as lights.



Blender room export process:
	
	1. Prepare visible meshes
		Visible mesh objects that should be exported:

			room_walls
			prop objects
			floor
			ceiling
			walls
			furniture
			environment meshes
			visible note/paper models

		Helper objects that should not be exported:

			Cam*_Pos
			Cam*_Target
			Cam*_Zone
			Door_*
			Door_*_Spawn
			Interact_*
			Col_*
			PlayerSpawn
			Light_*
			Light_*_Target
	
	2. Apply transforms

		For visible room meshes:
			Select the visible mesh objects.
			Press Ctrl + A.
			Choose Rotation & Scale.

		For helper objects:
			Select each trigger/collider/zone helper.
			Press Ctrl + A.
			Choose Rotation & Scale.

		This keeps exported bounds accurate.

	3. Export OBJ
		
		Select only the visible room geometry:
			room_walls 
			props 
			floor 
			ceiling 
			walls 
			visible environment objects 
			visible note/paper models

		Do not select helper objects:

			Cam*_Pos
			Cam*_Target
			Cam*_Zone
			Door_*
			Door_*_Spawn
			Interact_*
			Col_*
			PlayerSpawn
			Light_*
			Light_*_Target

		Then export:

			File -> Export -> Wavefront (.obj)

		Use these settings:

			Include:
				- Limit to Selected Objects / Selection Only: ON
				- Object Groups: optional
				- Material Groups: optional
				- Animation: OFF

			Transform:
				- Scale: 1.00
				- Forward Axis: -Z
				- Up Axis: Y

			Geometry:
				- Apply Modifiers: ON
				- Smooth Groups: optional
				- Write Normals: ON
				- Include UVs: ON
				- Write Materials: ON
				- Triangulate Faces: ON if available

			Path Mode:
				- Auto or Copy
				- If using textures, make sure texture paths are relative and the .mtl file is exported.

	4. Save paths carefully

		Example room folder:

			src/models/test_room02/

		Expected files:

			src/models/test_room02/test_room02.obj
			src/models/test_room02/test_room02.mtl
			src/models/test_room02/test_room02.json
			src/models/test_room02/textures/...

		The JSON should contain:

			"model": "src/models/test_room02/test_room02.obj"

		Make sure folder names, file names, and JSON model paths match exactly.

	5. Run the Blender-to-JSON script

		After exporting the OBJ, run the BlenderToJsonScript.py script.

		The script should export helper data into the room JSON:

			- playerSpawn
			- cameras
			- camera zones
			- wall colliders
			- extra Col_* colliders
			- Door_* triggers
			- Interact_* triggers
			- Light_* lights
			- ambientColor

		The helper coordinates are Blender coordinates.
		The C++ room loader converts them into engine coordinates.


	Texture checklist:
		If textures appear pink in Blender:
			- Relink missing textures.
			- Use File -> External Data -> Find Missing Files.
			- Then use File -> External Data -> Make Paths Relative.
		For engine loading:
			- Keep the .obj, .mtl, and textures together.
			- Prefer relative texture paths in the .mtl file.
			- Remember that Linux paths are case-sensitive.



Engine-Side rendering rule:
	
	If the OBJ is exported with:
		Forward axiz: -Z
		Up axis: Y

	the the room should be rendered with an identuty model matrix.

	Correct:
		glm::mat4 model = glm::mat4(1.0f); 
		shader.setMat4("model", model); 
		roomModel.Draw(shader);

	Incorrect:
		model = glm::rotate(model, glm::radians(-90.0f), glm::vec3(0,1,0));

	Extra render-time rotation will make the visual mesh disagree with:
		colliders
		cameras
		doors
		interaction zones
		light positions
		spotlight targets



Lightning debug hotkeys:
	
	The engine has debug controls for testing lights.
	
	Hotkeys:

		F5 = force one lightning flash 
		F6 = toggle lightning preview 
		F7 = toggle disable flicker 
		F8 = print light information once 
		F9 = treat spotlights as point lights

	Usage:
		For aiming lightning spotlights:

			F6 ON -> lightning is constantly visible for aiming
			F8 -> print light position/target/direction once
			F6 OFF -> return to real flicker timing
			F5 -> force a lightning flash

		For checking whether a spotlight issue is caused by cone direction:

			F9 ON -> spotlights behave like point lights
			F9 OFF -> spotlights use normal cone behavior


		If a light appears with F9 ON but disappears with F9 OFF, then the 
		light position/radius/intensity are fine 
		and the problem is the spotlight target, direction, or cutoff values.


Recommended workflow for window lightning:
	
	1. Add a light source:
		Light_Storm

	2. Add a target helper:
		Light_Storm_Target

	3. Place Light_storm near or outside the window.

	4. Put Light_Storm_Target on the wall/floor/area where the flash should land

	5. Custom properties for Light_Storm:
		type = spot 
		color = 0.45, 0.58, 1.0 
		intensity = 10.0 
		radius = 25.0 
		innerCutoff = 12.0 
		outerCutoff = 28.0 
		flicker = true 
		flickerType = lightning 
		flickerFrequency = 0.25 
		flickerStrength = 1.0 
		flickerRandomness = 1.0 
		flickerThreshold = 0.93

	6. Export JSON

	7. Run the engine

	8. Press F6 to keep lightning visible

	9. Adjusts Light_Storm_Target

	10. Turn preview off and test the flashing:
		F6 off
		F5 to force test flash



Current recommended workflow for note props:

	For visible note/paper items:

		1. Model a small paper mesh in Blender.
		
		2. Include the paper mesh in the OBJ export.
		
		3. Create an invisible interaction cube:
			Interact_TestNote

		4. Add note properties
			id = test_note 
			type = note 
			asset = src/ui/notes/test_note.png 
			repeatable = true

		5. Optionally - add a small point light near the note to point
			the viewers attention:

				Light_NoteGlow 
					type = point 
					color = 1.0, 0.85, 0.45 
					intensity = 0.8 
					radius = 2.0 
					flicker = true 
					flickerType = sine 
					flickerFrequency = 2.0 
					flickerStrength = 0.1

				This gives a simple collectible glow without needing an emissive material system yet.



Checklist for a blender model:

	Confirm that:
		
		- ROOM_ID is correct
		- CAMERA_NAMES in the script matches the Blender camera helpers 
		- ROOM_WALLS_OBJECT is correct
		- PlayerSpawn exists, otherwise a default value is used
		- Door targetRoom paths are correct
		- Door spawn helper namers match the door names
		- Interact_* helpers have correct custom properties
		- Light_* helpers have correct custom properties
		- Light_*_Target helpers are not exported as point lights and are named correctly
		- Scene ambientColor exists if custom ambient is desired
		- OBJ contains only visible geometry
		- JSON contains helper/gameplay data
		- Room renders with identity model matrix in C++
		- No extra C++ room roation is applied

